{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## MECHANISM CONFIRMED against Anthropic's documented limits + blast radius extended to CODE REVIEW\n\n### The provider documents this exactly — and AWS does not\n\nAnthropic's structured-outputs documentation (\"Schema complexity limits\",\nhttps://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs) states verbatim:\n\n> Structured outputs work by compiling your JSON schemas into a grammar that constrains Claude's\n> output. More complex schemas produce larger grammars that take longer to compile. To protect\n> against excessive compilation times, the API enforces several complexity limits.\n\n| documented limit | value |\n|---|---|\n| strict tools per request | 20 |\n| **optional parameters** (across strict tool + JSON output schemas) | **24** |\n| parameters with union types (`anyOf` / type arrays) | 16 |\n\n> Beyond the explicit limits ... there are **additional internal limits on the compiled grammar\n> size** ... When these limits are exceeded, you'll receive a 400 error with the message \"Schema is\n> too complex for compilation.\" ... **As a final stop-gap, the API also enforces a compilation\n> timeout of 180 seconds.**\n\n**That 180s is our failure.** Every one of the 27 observed failures took 180.6-185.9s. The two\ndistinct provider messages we see (\"Grammar compilation timed out\" and \"Schema is too complex\") are\nthe timeout stop-gap and the internal-limit check respectively.\n\nAnd the key causal statement:\n\n> **Reduce optional parameters.** ... **Each optional parameter roughly doubles a portion of the\n> grammar's state space.**\n\nAWS's Bedrock structured-output page documents the supported JSON Schema subset but **none** of\nthese limits, no timeout, and no error strings — it surfaces Anthropic's compiler failure wrapped as\n`ValidationException` without documenting what causes it. (AWS does document that successfully\ncompiled grammars are **cached for 24 hours**, which is why a schema can appear intermittent.)\n\n### Our contracts measured on the documented axis\n\n    contract                     optional params   union   native result\n    review_result                       10            9    OK        14.0s\n    completion_verdict                  14            9    OK        29.0s\n    verification                        22            0    FAIL     185.3s\n    plan_review_verification            31            0    FAIL     185.4s (also \"too complex\" 59.8s)\n    code_review_verification            36            0    FAIL     185.4s\n\nEvery field in the verification contracts carries a `default=` (deliberately — the docstring says an\nolder/absent verifier must ABSTAIN), so **every field is optional in JSON Schema**. That is what\nmakes these five contracts the expensive ones.\n\n**Note `verification` fails at 22 optionals — UNDER the documented cap of 24.** The published\nnumbers are necessary-but-not-sufficient; the effective ceiling is lower and interacts with nesting.\nOur measured boundary sits between **14 optionals (passes, 29.0s)** and **22 (fails)**. An\nindependent field report reproduces failure at 16 optionals with a schema SMALLER in bytes than a\npassing one — consistent with our own result that stripping descriptions (8,719 -> 2,403 bytes) did\nnot help.\n\n**Consequence for the fix: there is no single reliable published threshold.** That is why the chosen\ndesign is a MEASURED per-(model, contract) table plus a runtime fallback, not a computed bound.\n\n### BLAST RADIUS: the Gerrit `LLM-Review` gate is affected too — measured, not inferred\n\n`code_review_verification` (36 optionals) **fails natively in 185.4s**. It is bound by\n`src/rebar/llm/workflow/gates/code-review.yaml` at the Pass-2 `verify` step, which is\n`mode: structured` and pinned `model: standard`.\n\n`rebar.toml` resolves `standard = bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6` — the one model carrying\nthe native row. So **code-review's Pass-2 verification hits the identical defect**. The project\ndefault `[llm] model` is `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, which has NO native row and stays\nprompted — which is exactly why only `standard`-pinned steps fail and everything else looked fine.\n\n### How comparable OSS projects handle this (surveyed)\n\n* **pydantic-ai** (the library we use) has **no** native->prompted fallback anywhere. Its Bedrock\n  adapter forces `strict: true` on the output object, and `FallbackModel` swaps MODELS on transient\n  `ModelAPIError` — a 400 `ValidationException` is neither, so it propagates to the gate. It does\n  transform schemas (`BedrockJsonSchemaTransformer`) but only to strip unsupported KEYWORDS, never\n  to reduce complexity.\n* **None** of langchain-aws, instructor, llama_index, litellm, DSPy, Outlines or BAML implements an\n  automatic fallback on a schema rejection. Notably, **langchain-aws defaults to\n  `method=\"function_calling\"`** and makes native `json_schema` opt-in; **DSPy explicitly re-raises**\n  (`except LMError: raise`) before its JSON-mode fallback.\n* **The strongest counter-argument, worth recording.** TanStack/ai issue #682 is nearly our exact\n  case (~8KB schema, 50+ properties). They designed an `auto | native | tool` fallback, implemented\n  it, and **stripped it before merge** on three grounds: it hides a real signal that the schema is\n  too complex; it silently swaps a strict guarantee for a non-strict one; and it is machinery keyed\n  on provider error strings that can change.\n\n**How this fix answers those three objections** (the reason we are still doing it):\n1. *Hides the signal* — it will not. The fallback logs loudly and the rejection is classified\n   DISTINCTLY (AC1), so an operator sees \"this contract is too complex for this model\", not an\n   opaque outage. The measured table makes the fact permanent and reviewable.\n2. *Strict -> non-strict* — the delta is smaller here than for a generic library: rebar's prompted\n   path runs the same Pydantic validators, and `capabilities.py` already records that client-side\n   validators are MANDATORY on the native path anyway because Bedrock's schema transform strips\n   `ge`/`le` bounds. We were never relying on the provider for validity.\n3. *Brittle string matching* — mitigated by reusing the EXISTING in-tree precedent,\n   `translate_sampling_parameter_rejection` (`llm/failure.py:371`), which already conjoins\n   `status_code == 400` / `ValidationException` with a small phrase set for a different Bedrock 400.\n   Same shape, same seam, already tested.\n\nPractitioner practice outside frameworks does favour degrading: several projects drop the constraint\nand re-send on this exact error, one noting \"retrying the same constrained request would time out\nagain\". Retry-without-change is NOT a remedy for the over-ceiling case — matching our 27 identical\nfailures.\n\n### DECISION: AC3 closed as WON'T FIX (operator, 2026-08-05)\n\n> \"a single failed verify step no longer degrades every finding to `no-verification` ... its signing\n> decision reflects that distinction rather than failing all closed\"\n\n**Won't fix.** That degrade is deliberate, not defective: `plan_review_recovery` preserves the Pass-1\nfindings and routes them through `pass3_decide(None)`, the kernel's documented no-verification\ndegrade, and stamps `verify_failed: True`. Letting a review SIGN over findings that were never\nverified would weaken what a plan-review attestation means — the gate would certify unverified\nmaterial. The correct remedy is to make verification succeed, which is what this ticket now fixes.\nOnce the routing defect is repaired the degrade stops firing, because the step stops failing.\n\nThe other three ACs stand and are in scope."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785956982253850001,"uuid":"95a315a1-4c7b-4412-8511-4ee640ec0926"}